{% schema %}
{
  "name": "Why $29 can't buy it",
  "settings": [
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "heading",
      "label": "Heading",
      "default": "The math doesn’t leave room"
    },
    {
      "type": "richtext",
      "id": "body",
      "label": "Body copy",
      "default": "<p>Work backwards from the price tag.</p><p>A bottle listed at $29 on a marketplace leaves the seller somewhere <strong>around $9 to $12</strong> after platform fees, advertising, returns and shipping. <strong>That is the whole budget</strong> — metal, manufacture, cap, packaging, freight from Asia, and margin.</p><p>Now price the honest version. Machine-made copper bottles wholesale out of India at roughly $4 a unit, and that is the product category we are talking about: pressed on a line, sprayed inside, no testing. <strong>A bottle raised</strong> and hammered by hand is about an hour of somebody’s work. Batch testing at a <strong>laboratory</strong> is a real invoice per production run. Leaving the interior bare means accepting returns from customers who think tarnish is a defect, which costs money too.</p><p><strong>None of that fits inside $9.</strong></p><p>So something has to come out, and the order it comes out in is predictable. First the hand work — a die is faster and the listing can still say hammered. Then the testing, because nobody checks. Then the interior gets lacquered, because a bottle that never darkens generates fewer complaints. And if the number still does not work, the copper itself gets thinner, until it is a coating on something cheaper.</p><p><strong>That is not a conspiracy.</strong> Nobody in the chain is lying on purpose at every step. It is just what $29 buys once you subtract the fees, and the listing is written to make the subtraction invisible.</p><p>Which is also why the word copper does so much work in these titles. It is not a regulated claim on drinkware. It can legally describe a colour, a finish, a plating or a solid metal — four different products, one word, and the distinction buried in the specification block where nobody reads.</p>"
    }
  ],
  "presets": [
    {
      "name": "Why $29 can't buy it"
    }
  ]
}
{% endschema %}
